    "global index": "MMLU_formal_logic_29",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Use indirect truth tables to determine whether the following argument is valid. If the argument is invalid, choose an option which presents a counterexample. (There may be other counterexamples as well.)\nE \u2283 F\nG \u2283 ~F / ~G \u2228 ~E\n\nOptions: \nA. Valid\nB. Invalid. Counterexample when E, F, and G are true\nC. Invalid. Counterexample when E and G are true and F is false\nD. Invalid. Counterexample when F and G are true and E is false\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "C. Invalid. Counterexample when E and G are true and F is false  \nE = T, G = T, F = F: E \u2283 F = F, G \u2283 ~F = T, so premises not all true \u2192 not a counterexample  \nE = T, G = T, F = F makes first premise false, so invalidity must be checked via all premises true and conclusion false  \nE = T, G = T, F = F: E \u2283 F = F (premise false), skip  \nTry E = F, G = T, F = T: E \u2283 F = T, G \u2283 ~F = F (premise false)  \nOnly case with all premises true is E = T, G = F, F = F: E \u2283 F = T, G \u2283 ~F = T, conclusion ~G \u2228 ~E = T \u2228 F = T \u2192 valid here  \nE = F, G = T, F = F: E \u2283 F = T, G \u2283 ~F = T, conclusion ~G \u2228 ~E = F \u2228 T = T  \nWait: Try E = T, G = T, F = T: E \u2283 F = T, G \u2283 ~F = F (premise false)  \nCheck E = F, G = T, F = T: E \u2283 F = T, G \u2283 ~F = F (premise false)  \nOnly all-premises-true case: E = F, G = F, F = T: E \u2283 F = T, G \u2283 ~F = T, conclusion = T \u2228 T = T  \nE = F, G = F, F = F: E \u2283 F = T, G \u2283 ~F = T, conclusion = T \u2228 T = T  \nTry E = T, G = F, F = F: E \u2283 F = F (premise false)  \nWait\u2014counterexample requires all premises true and conclusion false. Conclusion ~G \u2228 ~E is false only if G = T and E = T. So only possible when E = T, G = T. Then for premises: E \u2283 F = T \u21d2 F = T; G \u2283 ~F = T \u21d2 ~F = T \u21d2 F = F. Contradiction\u2014no such assignment. So argument is valid.  \nBut wait: Option C says E and G true, F false. That makes E \u2283 F false, so premises not all true\u2014cannot be a counterexample.  \nOption D: F and G true, E false: E \u2283 F = T, G \u2283 ~F = F (since ~F = F) \u2192 second premise false. Not valid counterexample.  \nOption B: E, F, G all true: E \u2283 F = T, G \u2283 ~F = F \u2192 second premise false.  \nThus, no counterexample exists\u2014argument is valid.  \nA. Valid  \n\n\\boxed{A}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 209,
        "output_tokens": 631,
        "total_tokens": 840
