      "generated_answer": "The ellipse $ kx^2 + y^2 = 1 $ can be rewritten as $ \\frac{x^2}{1/k} + y^2 = 1 $, so semi-axes are $ a = 1 $ (vertical) and $ b = 1/\\sqrt{k} $ (horizontal).  \nSince $ k > 1 $, the major axis is vertical, and foci are at $ (0, \\pm c) $ with $ c = \\sqrt{1 - 1/k} $.  \nA circle through $ F_1, F_2 $ and tangent to ellipse at two x-axis points must be centered at origin with radius $ c $; tangency at $ (\\pm b, 0) $ implies $ c = b $, so $ \\sqrt{1 - 1/k} = 1/\\sqrt{k} $. Solving gives $ k = 2 $.  \n\\boxed{{2}}",
