    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7451",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: If $m$ and $n$ are odd integers, how many terms in the expansion of $(m+n)^6$ are odd?\n\nOptions: \nA. 6\nB. 3\nC. All the terms are odd\nD. 4\nE. 1\nF. 7\nG. 8\nH. 2\nI. 9\nJ. 5\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-30b-a3b-instruct-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Since $ m $ and $ n $ are odd integers, $ m + n $ is even, but we are interested in the parity (odd/even) of each term in the binomial expansion of $ (m+n)^6 $. Each term is of the form $ \\binom{6}{k} m^{6-k} n^k $. Since $ m $ and $ n $ are odd, their powers are odd, so the parity of each term depends only on $ \\binom{6}{k} $. The binomial coefficients $ \\binom{6}{k} $ for $ k = 0 $ to $ 6 $ are: 1, 6, 15, 20, 15, 6, 1. Among these, the odd ones are $ \\binom{6}{0} = 1 $, $ \\binom{6}{1} = 6 $ (even), $ \\binom{6}{2} = 15 $ (odd), $ \\binom{6}{3} = 20 $ (even), $ \\binom{6}{4} = 15 $ (odd), $ \\binom{6}{5} = 6 $ (even), $ \\binom{6}{6} = 1 $ (odd). So the odd coefficients are at $ k = 0, 2, 4, 6 $ \u2014 four terms.\n\nThus, four terms are odd.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 166,
        "output_tokens": 317,
        "total_tokens": 483
      },
      "provider": "local-vllm",
      "error": null
