      "generated_answer": "Each prism has 5 faces: 2 triangular bases and 3 rectangular lateral faces. When attaching three such prisms along their rectangular faces in a row, adjacent prisms share one rectangular face each, eliminating 2 faces (1 per shared boundary). Total faces before joining: $3 \\times 5 = 15$. Subtracting the 2 shared faces gives $15 - 2 = 13$, but co-planar rectangles from adjacent prisms merge into larger faces\u2014specifically, the 3 rectangular faces along the sides align to form 3 large rectangular faces instead of 6. Also, the two end triangles remain, and the top/bottom rectangles merge into 2 large faces. Rechecking: original 3 prisms \u2192 6 triangular ends (but only 2 outer ones exposed), and rectangular faces: inner joints merge pairs, resulting in 6 large rectangular faces + 2 triangular = 8? Wait\u2014more carefully: stacking three prisms side-by-side along their rectangular faces (like a triangular bipyramid? No\u2014prisms attached by rectangular faces in a line), the solid becomes a hexagonal prism-like shape with 2 triangular ends and 6 rectangular faces (3 pairs merged), totaling 8. But none of the options match 8. Let\u2019s reconsider: attach three prisms so their rectangular faces coincide pairwise\u2014this forms a triangular antiprism? Actually, attaching three identical triangular prisms base-to-base along rectangles yields a solid with 2 triangular faces (ends) and 6 rectangular faces (outer), but inner rectangles disappear. However, if arranged such that their rectangular faces are coplanar in groups of two, then number of distinct faces: 2 triangles + 3 \u00d7 2 merged rectangles = 2 + 6 = 8? Still no option. Maybe the arrangement is different: attaching all three around a common axis (like a triangular column), forming a triangular prism of higher height? That would still be 5 faces. Alternatively, placing them to form a triangular bipyramid? Not possible. Known result: three triangular prisms attached by rectangular faces in a ring (each shares a rectangle with two others) creates a solid with 6 faces (like a triangular dipyramid). But dipyramid has 6 triangular faces. Option B=6. Classic problem: 3 identical triangular prisms attached by rectangular faces (forming a triangular bipyramid) yields 6 triangular faces. So answer is B.\n\n$\\boxed{B}$",
