    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "I need to find the diameter-to-length ratio at which a log with specific gravity 2/3 transitions from floating horizontally to floating vertically.\n\nFor a cylinder floating vertically, stability requires that the metacentric height be zero at the transition point. Using the condition for neutral stability of a floating cylinder, the critical ratio occurs when the center of buoyancy equals the center of gravity.\n\nFor a cylinder with specific gravity s = 2/3 floating vertically, the critical diameter-to-length ratio is d/L = \u221a(2s/(1-s)) = \u221a(2(2/3)/(1-2/3)) = \u221a(4/3 \u00f7 1/3) = \u221a4 = 2.0.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
