uments are read by a high ranking British officer. He is amused by Talleyrand's role as Bonaparte's advisor and \u0102\u0160minence grise, a role that he finds plausible. However, he is especially puzzled by references to a British general named \"Wellington.\" In the final line of the story, the British officer is revealed to be Sir Arthur Wellesley - known in our reality as the Duke of Wellington. He attained the title by way of his victories in the Napoleonic Wars, which never took place in this universe.\n\nQuestion: How did Benedict Arnold's death change history?\n\nProvide your final answer in \\boxed{{}} format. Keep your explanation clear, concise, and within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Benedict Arnold's death at the Battle of Quebec led to British General John Burgoyne defeating Horatio Gates at the Battles of Saratoga. This caused the American Revolution to fail, preventing the formation of the United States. Without the American and subsequent French Revolutions, the Napoleonic Wars never occurred, drastically altering European and world history.  \n\\boxed{Benedict Arnold's death led to British victory at Saratoga, resulting in the failure of the American and French Revolutions and preventing the Napoleonic Wars.}",
